# secrets. The key belongs in the shell environment, never in the repo. DATA_CONTRACT.md §8: the key is
# read from the environment and is never written to disk or committed.
.env
.env.*
*.key

# macOS
.DS_Store

# local tooling config, not part of the project
.claude/

# python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
.pytest_cache/

# stored pages from captures. A full claude.ai page includes the sidebar, and therefore the titles of every
# other conversation, which are real queries from real work. DATA_CONTRACT.md §9 scrubs those before
# anything is committed, so the raw pages stay on the machine.
sayswho-page-*.html
pages/

# captures posted to the local audit server. They hold the answer text and the query behind it, which for
# the professional stratum is real work. They are inputs to the harness, not published artifacts.
captures/

# audits written by the local server. They embed the answer text and quoted spans from the pages fetched,
# which DATA_CONTRACT.md §9 keeps local. The writeup publishes verdicts and spans it has chosen, not a
# directory of everything ever run.
reports/

# fetched pages. DATA_CONTRACT.md §9: the repo publishes verdicts and quoted spans, not copies of the
# pages it fetched.
.cache/

# stored splits. A split is the answer broken into claims, so it carries the answer text, which for the
# professional stratum is real work. Same grounds as captures/. The split hash a gold set binds to is
# published in the run record; the claims themselves stay on the machine.
splits/

# hand-labelled gold sets. They hold the cited URLs and the passages the labeller pasted out of those pages,
# and for the professional stratum those are pages found by real work. DATA_CONTRACT.md §9 keeps quoted page
# text local unless the writeup chooses to publish a particular span. The agreement numbers computed from a
# gold set are publishable and the labels behind them stay on the machine.
goldset/

# run artefacts: results.json, readout.txt, the run record and the trace table. Same rule as reports/. The
# writeup publishes the numbers it has chosen and traced, not a directory of every run that ever happened,
# and a run over the professional stratum carries the answer text behind it. Written by the runner on every
# invocation, so leaving them untracked rather than ignored is how they end up in a git add -A.
runs/

# correspondence with the professor and the TA. Kept locally, not published.
email-to-professor.md
reply-to-professor.md

# video artefacts. The film, its B-roll and the raw screen recordings behind them are large binaries that
# nothing in the pipeline reads, and they are rebuilt from sources that are not in the repo anyway: the
# takes live on the machine they were shot on. `video/.gitignore` already covers `out` and `audio`.
video/public/footage/
*.mov
*.mp4

# generated narration and effects. Every one of them is rebuilt from the script in video/SOUND.md by the
# ElevenLabs voice named there, so the text is the source and these are the build output. video/.gitignore
# claims to cover this and does not: it ends at `out`.
video/public/audio/

# the videos the repo does keep, and the only ones. site/media/ holds the web-compressed cuts the site
# actually serves: the film at 3.9 MB rather than the 27 MB render, the B-roll at 1.5 MB, the install clip
# at 229 KB. Without them the site is three pages of prose with holes in it. The masters they were made
# from stay out: video/out/ is build output and the raw takes never entered the repo at all.
!site/media/
!site/media/*.mp4
!site/media/*.jpg

# the site's release archive, which tools/make_release_zip.py rebuilds from the tracked tree on demand.
# Committing it would mean a copy of the repo inside the repo, one commit stale from the moment it lands.
site/sayswho.zip
